{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Fabric Medallion Platform — Architect Review",
  "description": "A deep but focused walkthrough of BRZ, SIL, GLD, orchestration, quality gates, Direct Lake, and environment controls.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Orient on the whole system",
      "description": "The README presents a full lineage from source-aligned lakehouse data to decision products. The primary mechanism is a layer contract, not merely three storage names. That matters for ownership and testing; the gotcha is calling any sequence of tables a medallion architecture without defining responsibilities.",
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^# Fabric Medallion Supply Chain Platform$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the BRZ contract",
      "description": "BRZ preserves source grain and ingestion metadata while excluding report-specific logic. This makes replay and reuse possible. The gotcha is treating BRZ as ungoverned dumping ground—security, lineage, and freshness still apply.",
      "file": "docs/e2e-architecture.md",
      "pattern": "^## BRZ — source-aligned lakehouse$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Understand SIL responsibilities",
      "description": "SIL owns reusable references, snapshots, history, keys, and domain transformations. Working views separate transformation logic from materialized curated tables. The gotcha is pushing presentation-specific calculations into Silver and creating hidden report coupling.",
      "file": "docs/e2e-architecture.md",
      "pattern": "^## SIL — processing warehouse$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect the Gold serving contract",
      "description": "GLD publishes stable conformed dimensions and facts for Direct Lake. Separating Forecast Accuracy and Inventory Health marts keeps grains explicit while shared dimensions enable cross-domain consistency. The gotcha is allowing model-specific helper logic to destabilize the dimensional serving contract.",
      "file": "docs/e2e-architecture.md",
      "pattern": "^## GLD — serving warehouse$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare orchestration modes",
      "description": "The wrapper pipeline is operationally simple; the per-table graph improves isolation, parallelism, and recovery. Both express the same dependency model at different granularity. The gotcha is optimizing activity count without preserving idempotence and clear restart boundaries.",
      "file": "docs/orchestration.md",
      "pattern": "^## Domain wrapper pipeline$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Place quality before publication",
      "description": "Completeness, uniqueness, reconciliation, freshness, and drift gates block Gold publication when trust contracts fail. This protects every downstream consumer once. The gotcha is implementing quality only as report-side warning cards after bad data is already published.",
      "file": "docs/data-quality.md",
      "pattern": "^## Quality gates$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Study the architecture findings",
      "description": "The sanitized assessment highlights environment binding, object drift, orchestration, and measure-governance risks. Showing findings and controls demonstrates architecture review skill, not just solution drawing. The gotcha is publishing internal identifiers or blaming people instead of documenting reproducible controls.",
      "file": "docs/current-state-assessment.md",
      "pattern": "^## Sanitized control findings$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Review a portable layer contract",
      "description": "The YAML turns architectural intent into a machine-readable release input. CI can compare objects and bindings against it. The gotcha is treating a static manifest as truth without generating or validating it against deployed metadata.",
      "file": "architecture/layer-contracts.yaml",
      "pattern": "^architecture: fabric-medallion-supply-chain$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Close with the target-state story",
      "description": "You can now explain the end-to-end path, layer ownership, dependency waves, publish gates, and Dev/Prod controls. Continue with the semantic-model repository for relationship and metric governance, then the two report repositories for consumption design. Keep examples synthetic and never expose endpoints or proprietary definitions."
    }
  ]
}
